3. chess_camera.py
What it does:
Interfaces with a webcam to capture the current state of the chessboard.
Uses a perspective transformation to align the board correctly.
Detects the color of squares and generates a "colored board mask" representing piece locations.
Usefulness:
✅ Essential if working with live chessboard image processing.
❌ Not needed if using digital chess representations instead of real-world boards​
.

4. chessboard_perspective_transform.py
What it does:
Applies a perspective transformation to align the chessboard from a camera feed.
Uses machine learning techniques (polynomial regression) to calibrate the camera’s position.
Ensures that the chessboard appears correctly oriented for processing.
Usefulness:
✅ Critical for chessboard image recognition; required if using a camera.
❌ Useless if not dealing with real-world board images​
.

5. square_color_detector.py
What it does:
Uses a trained machine learning model (Support Vector Classifier) to determine the color of squares on a chessboard.
Loads PCA-transformed data for feature extraction.
Determines whether a square is occupied and by which color.
Usefulness:
✅ Necessary if using a camera to detect board state.
❌ Not needed for digital chess implementations​
.